  /* ======================================================================
     1.9 What the pixels are, and what the planet is doing
     ====================================================================== */

  /* ---- the key ----------------------------------------------------------
     Keane looked at the finished page and asked what the lines and the squares
     on the globe were, which is the first question any of this has to answer.
     Every layer already explains itself in an instrument card, but that is two
     clicks away behind a word ("Instruments") that does not obviously mean
     "what are these arcs". The key answers the question where the question is
     asked: on the picture, in the vocabulary of the pixels, one line per layer
     that is ACTUALLY BEING DRAWN, and every line is a real button into the card
     that owns it, so the short answer leads to the long one.

     Rows are decided from the same readouts the rack reads. A layer that is
     armed but has drawn nothing this minute gets no row: a key that describes
     arcs while there are no arcs is worse than no key at all. */
  /* r15 (6): one row per GLYPH the planet is drawing, and there are eight of
     them (arc, box, mark, dot, tick, arrow, ring, shade). The cap used to be
     five, which is where the tracks, the warnings and the station fell off
     the bottom and became marks the key could not name. The list scrolls
     inside a fixed maximum height, so the plate does not grow with it. */
  /* r19r (3): one row per MARK TYPE, which is twelve: arcs, thin arcs, beacon
     dots, boxes, marks, quake dots, small ticks, arrows, spreading rings and
     the three washes. The cap is what a class is allowed to be cut off by,
     and at eight the three rows this round SPLIT OUT would have been the
     three the cap dropped. The plate does not grow: the list scrolls inside
     its own ceiling and says how many rows it is showing. */
  var KEY_MAX_ROWS = 12;
  /* r19g (2): the list had ONE ceiling, 9.5rem, at every screen size, and it
     cut a row through the middle of a sentence under a hairline that reads as
     the end of the list: 142 px of key hidden at 1024x768 and still 52 px
     hidden at 1920x1080, so a bigger screen bought no more key. Two laws, both
     pure. The CAP is a share of the screen with a floor and a ceiling of its
     own (a tall screen may spend more of itself on the key; a short one may
     not spend everything). The HEIGHT is then snapped DOWN to a whole number
     of rows, so whatever the cap works out to, the last row a reader can see
     is a row they can read to its full stop. What did not fit is reached by
     scrolling, and the fade cue and the page's own thin scrollbar say so. */
